      "role": "user",
      "content": "That comparison makes sense. You mentioned that the DDC can get \"crowded\" and might struggle with the unique nature of non-book archival items. \n\nCould you expand on the specific technical risks of using a more rigid, decimal-based system for a specialized collection like this? I'm particularly concerned about what happens to the catalog's organization and findability when we try to force these highly specific 19th-century sub-topics into the broader, pre-existing DDC hierarchies. Does it lead to a kind of systemic \"disorder\" or chaos within the metadata?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Using a rigid decimal system like DDC for such a specialized collection is indeed risky, as it can lead to a \"systemic disorder\" where unique 19th-century sub-topics get forced into ill-fitting boxes, creating a chaotic metadata landscape. This happens because DDC's broad, generalist structure often lacks the granular flexibility needed for non-book archival items, forcing archivists to stretch or ignore specific details to fit the hierarchy. When you try to force these highly specific sub-topics into broader pre-existing hierarchies, you risk losing the nuance that makes these manuscripts valuable, effectively creating a \"goblin market\" where every item is shoved into a single, overcrowded stall regardless of its true nature.\n\nThis \"disorder\" manifests as a loss of findability, where users searching for niche topics might find nothing because the system has flattened the distinctions between similar but distinct items. It's like trying to organize a collection of rare, intricate clockwork automatons (the manuscripts) using only a standard shoebox (DDC); eventually, the automatons get crushed or mixed up, making it impossible to retrieve the specific piece you need. In contrast, the LCC's letter-number structure acts more like a vast",
      "source": "target",
